The MotorZ MPM aims to create a single MPM, that runs on all modern
Unix and Win32 platforms, by leveraging APR as much as possible.

The MotorZ MPM uses an APR Poll CB event system, with timers being
built in.  When an event, either an I/O or Timer is ready to run, it
is pushed to any available thread in the current process. A simple
APR Thread pool is used.

MotorZ uses Prefork as the framework and Simple for the actual event
structure.


Threading model
===============

Each child process runs:

  - N poll threads ("pollers", PollersPerChild; default: one per online CPU,
    capped). Each poller owns its OWN pollset, timer ring (+ guarding mutex)
    and lock-free transaction-pool recycle list, so pollers never contend with
    each other. A poller polls, dispatches ready I/O events and expired timers
    to the worker pool, and (for the listener-owning poller) accepts.
  - A shared APR thread pool of worker threads (ThreadsPerChild) that run the
    actual connection/request processing pushed to them; workers never poll.
  - A supervisor (the child's main thread) that watches MaxRequestsPerChild and
    the pipe-of-death / generation, signalling the pollers to wind down, then
    joins them on exit.

A connection is sharded to one poller at accept time (round-robin) and bound to
it for its whole lifetime: it re-arms in and times out on that poller's
pollset/ring. (Its transaction pool is recycled back to the ACCEPTING poller's
free-list, which is the single consumer of that list -- recycling is not
sharded, only I/O is.) Multiple pollers lift the old single-poll-thread
throughput ceiling: accept + event dispatch + timer expiry now scale with
PollersPerChild instead of being serialized on one thread.

Graceful stop
-------------

A child told to stop gracefully (AP_SIG_GRACEFUL, the pipe-of-death, a
generation change, or MaxConnectionsPerChild) does not exit at once. The
supervisor calls motorz_start_drain(): the child reports AP_MPMQ_STOPPING and
runs child_stopping, the listener-owning poller closes the listening sockets,
idle keep-alive connections are expired immediately, and connections still
writing or waiting on I/O run to completion. The child exits when conns_open
(connections accepted and not yet torn down) reaches zero. SIGTERM/SIGHUP
still exit immediately. The parent counts a draining child (quiescing) as gone
and starts its replacement in a free slot, so ServerLimit (default the
larger of 16 and 2 * StartServers) must leave room for several generations.
With every slot taken nothing can start, and with no child of the current
generation nothing accepts connections until a draining child exits; the
parent logs "scoreboard is full" (AH10657) once when that happens.

There is no child-side deadline on a drain: a connection still writing, or
waiting on I/O for an asynchronous module (CONN_STATE_ASYNC_WAITIO), is given
until it completes or its Timeout expires, so a graceful restart can take as
long as the longest such connection. (mpm_event behaves the same way.)
GracefulShutdownTimeout only bounds a graceful *stop* of the whole server, in
the parent.

Admission control (listener backpressure) keeps the child safe under overload:
when the worker pool saturates, the listener-owning poller removes the
listening sockets from its pollset (motorz_update_listeners /
motorz_disable_listeners) and re-adds them once the backlog drains, so the work
queue stays bounded. A child also stops accepting once it holds ThreadsPerChild
+ AsyncRequestWorkerFactor * (idle workers) connections not in lingering close
(motorz_connections_above_limit), as in mpm_event: connections parked in the
poller use no worker, so the worker counts alone do not limit them.

Scoreboard
----------

A connection is not bound to a thread, so the scoreboard's per-thread slots
stand for the worker-pool threads. Each worker thread takes a slot the first
time it runs a task, reports the connection it is processing through that slot
(SERVER_BUSY_READ/WRITE/KEEPALIVE/CLOSING, so mod_status shows busy workers),
and sets it back to SERVER_READY when the task ends. A connection parked in the
poller (keep-alive, write completion, lingering close, async wait) therefore
does not occupy a slot. The poller and supervisor threads have no slot.

Unsupported
-----------

ThreadStackSize is ignored (a warning is logged when it is set): the worker
threads are created by an APR thread pool, which offers no stack size control.
There is no accept mutex either; the listening sockets are non-blocking and
polled, so every child's listener-owning poller wakes for each new connection.

Relationship to mpm_event
-------------------------

MotorZ uses Prefork for process management and an APR thread pool for workers,
with pollers dispatching to that pool -- distinct from mpm_event's
listener/worker/fdqueue design where workers themselves re-arm a shared
thread-safe pollset. In practice, whether more pollers help depends on the
workload: if the worker threads are the CPU bottleneck (typical for real
request processing), the poll threads are not the limit and PollersPerChild
beyond 1-2 yields little. The multiple-poller design removes motorz's
*structural* single-thread ceiling, but per-box throughput is still governed by
worker CPU. For the broadest, most battle-tested high-concurrency async
behavior, mpm_event remains the reference; MotorZ stays a lean, self-contained
alternative.


HTTP/2 async handoff (ENABLED)
==============================

Status: motorz reports AP_MPMQ_IS_ASYNC = 1 and AP_MPMQ_CAN_WAITIO = 1
(MOTORZ_ENABLE_ASYNC = 1 in motorz.c). With async on, mod_http2 hands the
master connection (c1) back to the MPM between requests (CONN_STATE_ASYNC_WAITIO
in motorz_io_process()) instead of pinning a worker thread to it.

History: async used to be disabled because of intermittent dropped responses
under HTTP/2 connection churn. That was first attributed to a close-ordering
race in mod_http2 and "fixed" there; the real cause was in motorz and is
described below. MOTORZ_ENABLE_ASYNC remains a single flip point should a
regression ever appear.

Symptom
-------
Under HTTP/2 with many short-lived connections (the worst case is one request
per connection at high concurrency, e.g. `h2load -n 10000 -c 50 -m 1`), motorz
intermittently lost a few requests per run. h2load reports them as "Process
Request Failure". All h2 error codes are 0 (graceful), there is no TCP RST and
no 4xx/5xx. It is timing-sensitive: trace8 logging, lldb and Guard Malloc slow
the hot path enough to hide it; it reproduces at info level. mpm_event does not
show it.

Root cause: secondary connections shared the master's conn_state_t
-------------------------------------------------------------------
mod_http2 runs each stream on a secondary connection (c2) in its own worker
thread. The core creates a c2 as a copy of c1 (core_create_secondary_conn(),
h2_c2_create(): a memcpy of the conn_rec), so c2->cs pointed at c1's
conn_state_t. In motorz that is scon->cs, the very struct motorz_io_process()
reads to decide what to do next with the connection.

When a request on a c2 ends, the core writes the connection state through
c->cs (ap_process_request_after_handler(): WRITE_COMPLETION, or LINGER if the
c2 is marked aborted); h2_c2.c does the same. Because c2->cs was c1's, those
writes landed in the master's state:

  1. c1 sends a stream's last frame, the stream goes to CLEANUP, and
     mod_http2 aborts the c2 that is still "running" (h2_mplx.c, "c2 is
     running, abort"). That is harmless in itself: the response has been sent.
  2. c1 goes IDLE and returns CONN_STATE_ASYNC_WAITIO; motorz arms the socket.
  3. The c2 worker, still finishing, runs its end-of-request code. Aborted, it
     stores CONN_STATE_LINGER in the shared struct, over ASYNC_WAITIO.
  4. The client's next request makes the socket readable. motorz_io_process()
     finds LINGER, never re-enters the process_connection hooks, and
     lingering-closes the live connection: h2_session_pre_close() sends GOAWAY
     (reason='timeout', which only means "state was IDLE at close") and the
     request that was in flight is lost.

mpm_event does not have the problem because event_pre_connection() gives every
secondary connection its own conn_state_t (setup_slave_conn()). motorz had no
pre_connection hook.

The fix
-------
motorz_pre_connection() (motorz.c), registered APR_HOOK_REALLY_FIRST, gives a
secondary connection a private conn_state_t when it has none or shares the
master's, and clears the motorz module config that mod_http2 copies from c1, so
motorz_pre_read_request() cannot record a c2 request as the master's current
request (scon->r). The check mirrors event_pre_connection().

Measured with PollersPerChild 1, ThreadsPerChild 128,
`h2load -n 10000 -c 50 -m 1`, 20 runs each: before, 11 runs lost responses (63
lost, and only 194414 of 200000 connections started); after, 0 runs lost any
and all 200000 connections started. The earlier mod_http2 change (a graceful
client GOAWAY with streams in flight no longer goes straight to DONE, in
h2_session.c) was made under the close-ordering theory. It is not the cause of
this loss and was not shown to be needed; it was left alone.

Measuring it correctly (two pitfalls)
-------------------------------------
The committed tests learned two lessons the hard way; preserve them if you edit:

  1. Assert on RESPONSE LOSS, not on h2load's "failed" total. h2load's "failed"
     counts BOTH connection-establishment errors AND dropped responses:
       failed = (total - started)  +  (started - succeeded)
                \__ connection setup _/   \__ lost responses __/
     The first term can also be environmental (ephemeral-port exhaustion and
     accept-queue pressure when hammering loopback at high -c). Only the
     second term, started - succeeded, is response loss. The tests compute
     `lost = started - succeeded` and assert that is 0.

  2. Measure at LogLevel info, NOT trace8. This is a Heisenbug: trace8 slows
     the hot path enough to hide it, so a churn assertion run under trace8
     passes even with the fix removed -- i.e. it is vacuous. smoke.sh runs at
     trace8 for its state-machine traces, so its churn check is only a gross
     sanity pass; the load-bearing churn regression is the one in
     run-http2.sh, which runs at info.

How to reproduce it (if it ever comes back)
-------------------------------------------
  1. Remove the motorz_pre_connection() hook registration and rebuild.
  2. Config that maximizes the race (TLS vhost, `Protocols h2 http/1.1`):
       StartServers 1
       PollersPerChild 1        ; fewer pollers => more serialized, worse
       ThreadsPerChild 128      ; large, so it is NOT worker starvation
       ThreadLimit 128
       LogLevel info
  3. Drive maximum connection churn, one stream per connection:
       h2load -n 10000 -c 50 -m 1  https://localhost:PORT/
     Run it several times; about half the runs lose a few requests.
  4. To see the close path, use LogLevel trace2 (global; a per-module spec does
     not emit on this build) with small runs (n=2000) until one fails, then look
     for sessions that go "IDLE -- local goaway --> DONE" with GOAWAY
     reason='timeout'. On such a connection the closing thread's first action is
     h2_session_pre_close(), with no process_conn before it.
